---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import InsightCallout from '../../components/InsightCallout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/SectionHeading.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="LeadSuite 2.0"
  description="LeadSuite 2.0 is a ground-up redesign of the flagship law enforcement platform — and a new way of building it. I'm working as a hybrid designer-developer, using AI-assisted prototyping and a shared design system to move from idea to working product faster than a traditional handoff workflow allows."
  tags={['B2B SaaS', 'Design Systems', 'Design + Code']}
  heroImage="/images/projects/leadsuite2/thumbnail.png"
  accentColor="#1a1a2e"
  previousProject={{ title: 'ClearCase', slug: 'clearcase' }}
  nextProject={{ title: 'GriefShare', slug: 'griefshare' }}
>
  <div class="case-study-body">

    <!-- Challenge -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading title="The Challenge" />
        <div class="challenge-grid">
          <div class="challenge-item">
            <h3>The product challenge</h3>
            <p>LeadSuite and a second internal platform had diverged over time — different visual languages, different interaction patterns, different mental models. The goal is to unify them into a single coherent product that investigators can move through without context-switching.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="challenge-item">
            <h3>The process challenge</h3>
            <p>A redesign at this scale is too complex for traditional Figma handoff. By the time static specs reach engineers, the decisions behind them are already stale. The process itself needed to change alongside the product.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Shift -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="A Different Way to Build"
          subtitle="Instead of designing in Figma and handing off specs, I build working React prototypes that engineering can start from directly. The prototypes aren't throwaway artifacts — they're the first draft of the real product. This changes what collaboration looks like: instead of reviewing static screens, PMs and stakeholders interact with live flows and give feedback on real behavior."
        />
        <p class="pull-quote">"Instead of handing off Figma files, I hand off running branches."</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Approach 1 -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="AI-Assisted Prototyping"
          subtitle="I use AI coding tools to scaffold React components rapidly — turning design decisions into working UI in hours instead of days. This isn't about generating final code; it's about compressing the distance between idea and testable experience so decisions get made with real context, not hypothetical layouts."
        />
        <div class="img-placeholder" style="aspect-ratio: 16/9;" data-label="React prototype in VS Code / AI-assisted scaffolding · 16:9"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Approach 2 -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="Collaborative Exploration"
          subtitle="With a running prototype as the shared artifact, PM and design exploration happens in the same session. We're not reviewing a mockup — we're navigating a real flow, identifying friction in real time, and iterating on the spot. The feedback loop that used to take a week now takes an afternoon."
        />
        <div class="img-placeholder" style="aspect-ratio: 4/3;" data-label="PM + designer in prototype session · 4:3"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Approach 3 -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="Design System as Foundation"
          subtitle="Every prototype is built on the shared LeadsOnline design system — the same tokens, components, and patterns used across LeadSuite and ClearCase. This means prototypes don't just explore layout; they validate and extend the system in real context. What works gets promoted. What breaks reveals gaps worth fixing."
        />
        <div class="img-placeholder" style="aspect-ratio: 4/3;" data-label="Component library / system panel · 4:3"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Outcomes -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading title="Early Signals" />
        <div class="outcomes-grid">
          <InsightCallout
            title="Faster iteration"
            description="Ideas go from concept to testable prototype in days rather than weeks. The reduced cycle time means we catch wrong turns earlier and spend less time building toward the wrong thing."
          />
          <InsightCallout
            title="Clearer handoff"
            description="Engineers start from working React components, not annotated screenshots. The prototype is already opinionated about behavior, state, and structure — which means fewer clarifying questions and faster builds."
          />
          <InsightCallout
            title="Earlier alignment"
            description="PM and leadership see real flows before the full build begins. Decisions that used to get made mid-sprint — because a static mockup didn't surface the edge case — now happen in the prototype session."
          />
          <InsightCallout
            title="Stronger system"
            description="Every prototype is a stress test for the design system. Components that hold up get promoted; gaps that surface get filled. The system is more robust because it's being used, not just maintained."
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .challenge-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .challenge-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .challenge-item {
    padding: var(--space-xl);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .challenge-item h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-h4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .challenge-item p {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .pull-quote {
    font-size: var(--text-h3);
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    color: var(--color-text);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding-left: var(--space-xl);
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
  }

  .outcomes-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .outcomes-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  /* Placeholder image scaffolding */
  .img-placeholder {
    width: 100%;
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border: 2px dashed var(--color-text-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .img-placeholder::after {
    content: attr(data-label);
    position: absolute;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-align: center;
    padding: var(--space-md);
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { initCaseStudyAnimations } from '../../scripts/scroll-animations';
  initCaseStudyAnimations('.case-section');
</script>
